course: world
lesson: 166
title: "Everyone has one!": the crowd is not a reason
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Zara wants the new light-up trainers. "Mum, EVERYONE has them." Mum asks who. Zara counts: Amira, Jack... and that's it. Two kids out of thirty. "Everyone" turned out to be two.
explain: The bandwagon trap says: lots of people do it or say it, so it must be right. But a crowd can copy one loud voice, like the whole class calling the new girl "weird" because one kid said so on day one, when nobody had talked to her.
explain: Sometimes the crowd doesn't even know what it thinks. At the school disco nobody dances because nobody is dancing; one girl starts and within a minute everyone is, and afterwards they all say, "I wanted to dance the whole time."
explain: The fix is two questions. Who exactly? Count them. And even if it were everyone: is it a good reason for me? Oscar says nobody reads at home, but the chart says 24 out of 30 do, and reading would still help him if nobody did.
explain: The playground takes the mickey out of the claim, not the kid: "Everyone, is it? Two out of thirty? That's not everyone, that's Amira and Jack."
fact: The bandwagon trap, also called "everyone does it" or appeal to popularity, treats how many people do or say something as proof that it is right.
fact: Count before you say "everyone": two out of thirty is not everyone.
fact: Even if everyone did something, that would not on its own be a reason for you to do it.
fact: Many people repeating something they all heard from one person is still just one person's claim.
fact: Your friends are not a fair sample of everyone; neither is asking only people on your side.
fact: If the only voice saying something has something to gain, like a boy selling his cards, be extra careful.
fact: Not being invited to a party usually has a simple reason, like a small home, and is often not about you at all.
fact: In a crowd, people often wait for each other and guess wrongly what the others want; one person going first can show what everyone really wanted.
fact: If you don't know what a word means, ask a trusted adult or look it up before using it.
fact: Spiders do not lay eggs in people's ears; "everyone knows" is not a source.
fact: Playground equipment is checked for safety before children use it.
fact: Clubs are for fun and learning, so the best choice depends on what the child enjoys, not on what other families do.
fact: Words people use: "lowkey" means a bit or secretly, "highkey" means very or openly, "shinies" are rare shiny cards, "iconic" means legendary, "case closed" means the matter is settled (here said sarcastically), and "deadly" here means scary-dangerous.
exercise: Zara says everyone has the light-up trainers. How many do, out of thirty? | Two | Twenty | Thirty | Fifteen
exercise: Charlie says Pokemon cards are "out" because everyone says so. Where did he hear it? | From one boy, Kyle, who had just sold all his cards | From a survey of the whole of Year 5 | From the card company's website | From his teacher in assembly
exercise: Why should Charlie be extra careful about Kyle's claim? | Kyle had something to gain from saying it | Kyle is younger than Charlie | Kyle doesn't like Pokemon | Kyle said it on a Monday, when everyone is grumpy about school
exercise: The class calls the new girl "weird" because someone said so on day one. What is missing? | Anyone actually talking to her | A vote of the whole class | A letter from her old school | Proof she is from another town
exercise: Max says everyone's going to Ellie's party except you, so you did something wrong. How many are invited? | Six | Everyone but you | Thirty | Twelve
exercise: Oscar says nobody in his class reads at home. What does the teacher's chart show? | 24 out of 30 children read at home last week | Only Oscar reads at home | Nobody reads at home except the teacher | 3 out of 30 children read at home
exercise: Ava uses a word from TikTok because everyone does, and gets told off. What should she have done first? | Asked a trusted adult or looked up what it means | Used it only in the playground | Waited until lots more people in her class started using it too | Said it more quietly
exercise: Jack says his class is the best because everyone in his class says so. What is wrong? | He only asked people on his own side | He didn't ask the class loudly enough | His class is much too small to be the best | Classes can never be compared at all, ever
exercise: At the disco nobody dances, then one girl starts and everyone joins. What does this show? | People wait for each other and guess wrongly what others want | Nobody really wanted to dance at all | The girl forced everyone to dance | Discos always start slowly on purpose, so people save their energy for later
exercise: Sam says spiders lay eggs in your ear "because everyone knows that". What is true? | Spiders don't lay eggs in people's ears | Spiders lay eggs in ears, but only at night when you're asleep | It happens to one in ten people | Only big spiders do it
exercise: Freddie's mum signs him up for football because "everyone" at the school gate is. What should decide instead? | What Freddie enjoys, like drawing | What the most mums say | Which club is closest to school | Which club his friends' dads like
exercise: Leila says the new slide is deadly because three kids said so. How many of them have been on it? | None | All three | Two of them | Just one
exercise: What do grown-ups call "everyone does it, so it's right"? | The bandwagon | A fair sample | A hasty excuse | A scientific test
exercise: What are the two best questions when someone says "everyone"? | Who exactly, and is it a good reason for me? | How loud were they, and how old are they? | When did they say it, and where? | Are they cool, are they my friends, and would they ever lie to me?
exercise: Why are your own friends not a fair way to find out what "everyone" does? | They are a small group, often alike | Friends always lie about such things | Friends never read at home | Friends are older than everyone else
test: Dad: "If everyone jumped off a cliff..." Zara: "Here we go." What is Dad's point? | Lots of people doing something doesn't make it a good idea | Zara should go cliff jumping with friends | Cliffs are where trainers are tested | Everyone in Zara's class jumps off cliffs at the weekend, so it must be fine
test: His friend: "Kyle sold his cards, so now cards are dead? Bro is lowkey just trying to get rid of his last shinies." What does "lowkey" mean here? | A bit, or secretly | Very loudly | In a low voice | Not at all
test: Teaching assistant: "Thirty kids saying it once is still just one kid saying it." What does she mean? | Repeating one person's claim doesn't make it more true | Thirty kids are louder than one | Only one kid at a time should be allowed to talk in the classroom | The new girl is only one person
test: Rosie: "She invited six people, mate. Her flat is the size of a shoebox. I didn't do a crime." Which is true? | Not being invited often has a simple reason | Rosie must have done something wrong | Everyone else was invited | Small flats aren't allowed to have parties with more than six people
test: Teacher: "Nobody? 24 people did. You're the nobody, Oscar." The class: "OOOOH." What is the teacher really correcting? | Oscar's claim, by counting the facts | Oscar's reading level | Oscar's friends for not reading | The class for being noisy
test: Her best mate: "You said it to the HEAD teacher, Ava. Lowkey iconic, highkey detention." What is the lesson? | Don't copy a word just because everyone uses it | Only say rude words to teachers you like | Detention is iconic | The head teacher doesn't know any slang
test: Kid from the other class: "Yeah and my mum says I'm handsome. Wow, case closed." Why is this a good comeback to Jack? | Asking only people on your side proves nothing | Mums are always right about looks | Jack's class really is the best | His mum is a fair judge of classes, because she has seen both of them
test: What would happen at the disco if one person started dancing straight away? | Others would likely join, since many wanted to | Everyone would leave the disco | Nobody would ever dance again after that, because it would be embarrassing | The music would have to stop
test: Mia: "Everyone knows? Name one person who's had spider babies in their ear. One. I'll wait." What is she asking for? | A real source instead of "everyone knows" | A spider to show the class | Proof that Sam is lying on purpose to scare the younger children | A doctor's note about ears
test: Freddie: "Mum, if all the mums at the gate signed their kids up for ballet, would I have to do ballet?" Mum: "...Don't tempt me." What is Freddie showing? | Choosing a club because others do is not a good reason | Ballet is better than football | All mums secretly like ballet | Freddie secretly wants to do ballet instead of football or drawing
test: Mo: "Have you been on it?" "No." "Has anyone?" "No." "Then how is it deadly, Leila? It's a slide, man." What turned out to be true? | The slide was just a bit fast | The slide was really dangerous | The slide was closed for repairs | Nobody was allowed on the slide
exam: Identify the principal error in the assertion that a product ought to be acquired because "everyone" possesses it. | Popularity is offered in place of a reason, and the claim was not counted | The product was not sufficiently expensive | The assertion was made on a school day | The product was available only in one colour
exam: Determine the number of pupils, out of a class of thirty, who were in fact found to possess the trainers in question. | Two | Thirty | Twenty-eight | Fifteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning spiders and the human ear. | Spiders do not lay their eggs in human ears | Spiders customarily lay eggs in the ears of sleepers | Spiders do so only during the winter months | The matter is settled by common knowledge
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises an opinion gathered exclusively from members of one's own class? | A biased sample which cannot settle a comparison | A fair and representative survey | A scientific measurement of quality | An independent judgement from outside
